/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a sequence x[n] that repeats every N samples: x[n+N] = x[n].
   - X[k] = Σn=0N−1 x[n] e−j2πkn/N
   - X[k] is the coefficient of harmonic k; x[n] is one period; N is the period in samples; n runs from 0 to N−1.
   - x[n] = (1/N) Σk=0N−1 X[k] ej2πkn/N
   - The 1/N factor is the convention used here; k runs over the N harmonics.
   - The analysis equation gives X[k] from one period.
   - Only N distinct exponentials exist.
   - For real x[n], X[N−k] = X*[k].
   - Holds when x[n] is real; X*[k] is the complex conjugate of X[k].
   - Parseval's relation fixes average power: Pavg = (1/N) Σn=0N−1 |x[n]|² = (1/N²) Σk=0N−1 |X[k]|².
*/
