/* book exam traps: Reading k past N−1 and expecting zero: X[N]=X[0], X[N+1]=X[1], and so on. The coefficient sequence is periodic. | Putting the 1/N factor on the wrong equation. Some books put it on analysis, so X[0] becomes the average. State the convention before computing. | Forgetting that with 1/N on synthesis, X[0] is the sum of one period, not the average. For the pulse train X[0]=3, not 0.375. | Using continuous-time harmonic counting. A sampled period has only N distinct frequencies; k=N/2 is the highest for even N. | Applying X[N−k]=X*[k] to a complex-valued x[n]. The conjugate symmetry holds only for real sequences. */
