/* book: lead: A periodic sequence has a line spectrum: a few frequencies, each with a coefficient. An aperiodic sequence has no period to pick out those lines, so its spectrum becomes a continuous curve X(ejω) of frequency. That curve is the discrete-time Fourier transform (DTFT). It exists for every absolutely summable sequence, it repeats every 2π radians per sample, and one period holds all the information.  */
/* book Figure 6.2.1: For x[n] = (0.8)ⁿu[n] the magnitude is 5 at ω = 0, 0.781 at π/2 and 0.556 at π; the pattern repeats every 2π. With a = 0.5 the peak is only 2, so a pole nearer 1 gives a taller, narrower peak. */
