/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a sequence x[n] defined for every integer n.
   - X(ejω) = Σₙ₌₋∞∞ x[n] e−jωn
   - x[n] is the sequence; ω is frequency in rad/sample; the sum runs over every integer n.
   - The inverse turns the curve back into the sequence.
   - x[n] = (1/2π) ∫₂π X(ejω) ejωn dω
   - the integral runs over any interval of length 2π in ω; n is an integer.
   - Because n is an integer, e−j(ω+2π)n = e−jωn e−j2πn = e−jωn.
   - The sum converges uniformly, and X is a continuous function of ω, when x[n] is absolutely summable: Σ|x[n]| < ∞.
   - Some useful sequences are not absolutely summable.
   - The rectangular pulse gives a Dirichlet kernel, not a sinc.
*/
