/* book exam traps: Writing X(jω) or X(ω). The DTFT is X(ejω); the argument is a point on the unit circle and ω is in rad/sample, not rad/s. | Treating X(ejω) as aperiodic and quoting one branch. The curve repeats every 2π, so the value at ω = 3π/2 equals the value at ω = −π/2. | Using 1/(1 − e−jω) as the whole DTFT of u[n]. The impulses of area π at ω = 2πk are missing, and the expression is infinite there anyway. | Forgetting the 1/2π in multiplication and in Parseval. Both come straight from the inverse transform; the convolution property itself has no such factor. | Mixing up DTFT and DFT. The N-point DFT is N samples of X(ejω), not X itself; a feature between the samples is invisible (6.3). */
